Under `--modular-termination` (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/211), any trait may now be extended from any module, without `{:termination false}` or `@AssumeCrossModuleTermination`. A member with a `decreases {:global}` bound binds every implementation. A foreign implementation of a member without one must be a leaf, which reaches no dispatched call and no application of a function value whose code is not known, or have a bound of its own, declared or inherited from a subtrait, which is checked like any other. A dispatched call to a member without a bound is TOP, so only a member without a bound may make it. A member that every implementation should be able to call is given a low bound, such as `decreases {:global} 0`. The legacy forms keep their unchecked meaning for members without a bound. Without the option, nothing changes. The bound check applies to every call, including calls of extreme and prefix predicates and co-calls, and a least or greatest lemma's body runs under the lemma's bound. A function, method or lemma without a body is taken to reach a dispatched call unless it promises `{:termination_closed}`, since a refining module may give it a body that the code it inherits was not checked against.
